WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.210 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  skeeball is my life I met my wife  playing skee-ball skee-ball has had a  material impact on my life and I want to  share that with others and this space  will be a conduit for that joie Mucha is  a three-time skee-ball national champion  and the owner of joie de ket an arcade  rental repair and events company that he  started in 2010 building my business  started out as a side project to my day  job I bought at that skee-ball machine  on eBay to practice and then had to  learn how to repair it so the natural  progression was to put it in a bar see  if it could make some recurring revenue  and then use that money to buy another  skee-ball machine in 2014 Mucha  purchased this warehouse in San  Francisco's Mission District as a repair  and storage facility in 2019 he decided  to convert it into an arcade and  restaurant which should have been a  straightforward process since the  property was already zoned for  commercial use but a community activist  incurring nothing more than a six  hundred dollar fee was able to  temporarily scuttle mooches plans  forcing him to spend months rallying  community support and pleading his case  at a public hearing Joey the cat's new  outpost is slated to open in spring of  2021 but the story of how the project  was nearly stopped in what Miuccia  endured in order to prevail underscores  how even the most benign land-use  changes in San Francisco can be hampered  by red tape it's a root cause of the  city's housing crisis and jeopardizes  San Francisco's competitiveness over the  long term Joey the cat should be renamed  Joey the liar  this isn't within 500 feet of an  elementary school we're talking about  proximity to schools we don't need other  people walking by that and seeing these  negative toxic images I'm not converting  this to a private event space only for  corporates I'm converting this to a  public space that can be used by all in  a perfect world if there was no red tape  we would have been open six months ago  muchas troubles started when Kevin Ortiz  a local activist affiliated with the  anti gentrification group Cultural  Action Network filed an application for  discretionary review a process through  which any member of the public can ask  the San Francisco Planning Commission to  review an application for a building  neither ortiz nor cultural Action  Network responded to our interview  requests even though these reviews are  supposed to be used only under  exceptional and extraordinary  circumstances they've become a regular  part of the city's land use process for  example the owner of a falafel  restaurant was able to delay the opening  of a competitor by claiming that the  neighborhood already had enough falafel  this homes third floor addition was  blocked on the grounds that it wasn't  sufficiently Victorian the conversion of  a laundromat to an apartment building  has been delayed for over five years due  in part to its casting a shadow on an  already shaded school yard so far in  2019 the Planning Commission publicly  heard 91 requests for discretionary  review because every building permit in  the city is subject to this process  it adds enormous cost to construction  exacerbating the city's severe housing  shortage  Miuccia prevailed by devoting enormous  time to winning over community support  which otherwise would have gone into  running his business I've basically  become a politician which I don't want  to become and I don't want that for  anyone I've watched a bunch of Planning  Commission hearings and research to kind  of understand the concept because small  business owners shouldn't be flung into  this process and expected to be perfect  at this when the activists are going to  these we almost weekly I've gotten a  profile and all the different planning  commissioners I know their names and I  know kind of what they vote for and  against my team and I went and garnered  as much support as we could from the  community and we've worked with  architects and had to figure out how to  make this space work for both what I  want it to be and what is legally  permitted and make good business sense  out of it and finding that triangulation  is incredibly complex and challenging  and has been very burdensome process for  a small business owner  Miuccia went door-to-door in the mission  asking for help from his neighbors at  the hearing more than twice as many  supporters his opponents came out to  speak joy the cat is fun for both adults  and children and more importantly it is  kept clean and it is a safe environment  which is more than I can say for some of  the parks and our neighborhood political  connections also came into play  Miuccia received a letter of support  from California State Treasurer Fiona MA  who once held a fundraiser at Joey's  arcade on a principled level I think  it's so hard for a small business owner  to be successful in this city and when  you start to layer on red tape for these  hurdles and roadblocks that come out of  personal interests or community  interests that don't necessarily align  with the business it makes it very  difficult and I think it's something  that should be changed at a policy level

